# Wallapop Tracker

Tracks Wallapop (Spain) listings for whatever you configure it to hunt for. It fetches listings daily, stores every raw response and every price change in a local SQLite database, extracts specs from titles and descriptions, and flags bargains and suggests lowball offers.

It is **read-only**: it searches and reads public listings only. No login, no messaging sellers, no buying.

## What it tracks is up to you

There are no hardcoded products. You define **targets** — the things you want to find — entirely in `targets.toml`. Each target lists its search queries, its extraction rules (regex/keyword dictionaries), and a bargain rule. Adding a target is a config edit, no code changes. See [`targets.example.toml`](targets.example.toml) for a worked example.

## How it works

- `tracker run` fetches each configured query through [wallapop-cli](https://github.com/Microck/wallapop-cli), one page per process, at least 3 s between calls.
- Raw responses are stored untouched (`raw_response`); listings and observations are normalised (`listing`, `observation`). History is append-only — a listing's price over time is never overwritten.
- Rule-based extractors pull specs from title and description, configured per target in `targets.toml`.
- A scorer computes bargains and lowball offers per target from the accumulated history.
- Seller reputation is fetched once per seller (`wallapop user show`): rating, review count, sold count, verified flag and account age — the signals you want for spotting scams.

## Requirements

- Python 3.13+
- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)
- [wallapop-cli](https://github.com/Microck/wallapop-cli), installed as `wallapop` on your `PATH`

No Python dependencies: the standard library + SQLite only.

## Install

```sh
git clone https://github.com/n0nuser/wallapop-tracker
cd wallapop-tracker
cp targets.example.toml targets.toml   # then edit it
```

## Configure

Edit `targets.toml` (start from `targets.example.toml`). `[defaults]` holds the guardrails and the origin point used to compute distance. Each `[targets.<name>]` table holds its queries, bargain rule and extraction rules.

## Run

```sh
uv run tracker run
uv run tracker report    # bargains and thresholds per target
```

One run a day is enough. Options:

```sh
uv run tracker run --db my.db        # write to a different database file
uv run tracker run --replay DIR      # read fixtures from DIR instead of the network
```

## Test

```sh
uv run python -m unittest discover -s tests
```

## Documentation

- [`docs/GLOSSARY.md`](docs/GLOSSARY.md) — the terms (Target, Listing, Observation, Bargain, Lowball…).
- [`docs/adr/`](docs/adr/) — design decisions: stack, storage schema, anti-bot posture, wallapop-cli, dashboard.
- [`docs/api.md`](docs/api.md) — background notes on Wallapop's internal API (the collector goes through wallapop-cli).

## Behaviour and guardrails

- Read-only: `wallapop search` and `wallapop item show` only.
- No proxy, no login, no challenge solvers.
- At least 3 s between calls, capped requests per run.
- On a block (rate-limited or a CloudFront challenge), the run stops and marks nothing as sold/removed.
